    /// en: "Your messages never reach the distributor or its server. Each wake is encrypted to this phone and carries nothing; Rún fetches your messages over Tor and decrypts them after you unlock."
    static var notifEduUpContentSafeTitle: String { NSLocalizedString("notif_edu_up_content_safe_title", value: "Your messages never reach the distributor or its server. Each wake is encrypted to this phone and carries nothing; Rún fetches your messages over Tor and decrypts them after you unlock.", comment: "") }
    /// en: "The distributor app keeps one connection open to a push server and hands each wake to Rún. Nothing about your messages changes: the wake carries no message, and Rún still fetches and decrypts everything over Tor after you unlock."
    static var notifEduUpDistributorDeep: String { NSLocalizedString("notif_edu_up_distributor_deep", value: "The distributor app keeps one connection open to a push server and hands each wake to Rún. Nothing about your messages changes: the wake carries no message, and Rún still fetches and decrypts everything over Tor after you unlock.", comment: "") }
    /// en: "This phone has no Google services, so a separate app you choose — a UnifiedPush distributor such as ntfy — delivers the wake signal."
    static var notifEduUpDistributorTitle: String { NSLocalizedString("notif_edu_up_distributor_title", value: "This phone has no Google services, so a separate app you choose — a UnifiedPush distributor such as ntfy — delivers the wake signal.", comment: "") }
    /// en: "That is one party more than the Google build has. The distributor sees every wake because it delivers them; its push server sees them because it forwards them and, on a public server, also sees this phone’s IP address. Neither sees a sender, a message or a count. Tor normally hides that timing; notifications trade some of it for instant delivery. Run your own push server and the second party is you."
    static var notifEduUpPartiesDeep: String { NSLocalizedString("notif_edu_up_parties_deep", value: "That is one party more than the Google build has. The distributor sees every wake because it delivers them; its push server sees them because it forwards them and, on a public server, also sees this phone’s IP address. Neither sees a sender, a message or a count. Tor normally hides that timing; notifications trade some of it for instant delivery. Run your own push server and the second party is you.", comment: "") }
    /// en: "Two parties learn when you are contacted — the distributor app and its push server — never who wrote or what. Those timestamps alone can show when you communicate."
    static var notifEduUpPartiesTitle: String { NSLocalizedString("notif_edu_up_parties_title", value: "Two parties learn when you are contacted — the distributor app and its push server — never who wrote or what. Those timestamps alone can show when you communicate.", comment: "") }
    /// en: "The relay sends a contentless wake through %1$s — no sender, no text, no count — and Rún shows a generic “Notification”. Nothing is fetched or decrypted until you open Rún and unlock. %3$s works the same way; neither vendor is more private.\n\nThe relay learns something too: all your inboxes register under the one push address %2$s issued to this phone, so the relay can tell which conversations share a device. Someone able to compel both %2$s and the relay could link you to your conversations. That is why this switch is off until you turn it on."
